00:00:28.028 --> 00:00:29.904
- [Master Tesfatsion:] So, who we got in the pool hall today?

00:00:29.904 --> 00:00:32.615
Oh we got a Hall of Famer, my man Cris Carter.

00:00:32.615 --> 00:00:33.241
- [Cris Carter:] What's up, baby?

00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:33.867
- How you living, brother?

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:34.576
- How you doing, man?

00:00:34.576 --> 00:00:36.703
- Nice at a lot of things. Let's see how nice you are at pool.

00:00:36.703 --> 00:00:38.913
- My hands is nice. They just ain't nice at pool.

00:00:38.913 --> 00:00:40.999
- [Laughing]

00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:42.500
- Come on. I’m all heart, man.
- You know a little bit about pool though.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:43.626
- Oh! Come on, get in there!

00:00:43.626 --> 00:00:45.211
- Solid break, solid break.

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:46.337
So, when did you start playing football?

00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:47.964
- I started playing football at eight years old.

00:00:47.964 --> 00:00:48.673
- Eight years old?

00:00:48.673 --> 00:00:52.135
- I tried to play when I was seven. Me and my cousin went down to the park

00:00:52.135 --> 00:00:57.557
and he was like, "Man, I'll doctor up the birth
certificate to make you born a year earlier."

00:00:57.557 --> 00:00:59.851
I was trying to play when I was seven. And the lady was like,

00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:03.104
"I think you need to bring your mom back."
I was like, "OK. I'll go home and get her."

00:01:03.104 --> 00:01:04.856
A year later we didn't come back till.

00:01:04.856 --> 00:01:06.441
- [Laughing]

00:01:06.441 --> 00:01:09.277
- So yeah. That's my first experience with football was eight years old.

00:01:09.277 --> 00:01:11.946
First time I touched a ball in a game, I scored a touchdown.

00:01:11.946 --> 00:01:13.948
- At that point, it was just- it was history.

00:01:13.948 --> 00:01:17.494
OK. So, young kid, Middletown, Ohio. What gravitates you to football?

00:01:17.494 --> 00:01:21.289
- I loved the contact, man. I grew up a better basketball player

00:01:21.289 --> 00:01:26.461
in high school until I turned 16, 17. Then, my
recruitment, I was All American in both,

00:01:26.461 --> 00:01:28.713
but I was first team All American in football.

00:01:28.713 --> 00:01:32.092
So in the process of trying to decide where I was gonna go to school,

00:01:32.092 --> 00:01:35.804
I was only going to be recruited by schools who would let me play both.

00:01:35.804 --> 00:01:39.182
So that's one of the reasons why I chose Ohio State.

00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:41.768
- [Balls hitting. Ball dropping in socket.]

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:44.646
- OK. I’m so glad man we didn’t play for any money.

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:45.438
- [Laughing]

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:46.272
- OK!

00:01:47.023 --> 00:01:49.609
- You get to Ohio State. What was that experience like and

00:01:49.609 --> 00:01:51.986
what was going on in the background?

00:01:51.986 --> 00:01:55.990
- That beginning was just not real good. And talking to my buddies,

00:01:55.990 --> 00:01:58.284
they were talking about quitting and going back home.

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:00.662
And I was like, "Man, I can't go back to the crib."

00:02:00.662 --> 00:02:02.413
- Like, "I can't go back."
- Why? Why was that not an option? 

00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:05.750
- When you grow up in a small town,
man, regardless of how good you are,

00:02:05.750 --> 00:02:09.921
there are people that are always
trying to hold you back or say things.

00:02:09.921 --> 00:02:10.421
- Mhmm.

00:02:10.421 --> 00:02:13.299
- "You ain't gonna never make it. You ain't gonna be nothing."

00:02:13.299 --> 00:02:16.511
But, man, you get those voices, man. Everyone gets them. 

00:02:16.511 --> 00:02:20.431
- For sure. What was it like for you in the
'80s, dealing with these ramifications

00:02:20.431 --> 00:02:22.517
and these different stipulations of a different time?

00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:26.521
- Well, it was totally against the rules, any type
of benefit for any type of student athlete.

00:02:26.521 --> 00:02:30.275
So you didn't have the ability to work. You also didn't have the ability

00:02:30.275 --> 00:02:33.444
to be able to take any type of endorsements or anything like that.

00:02:33.444 --> 00:02:34.946
It was totally different. Today’s athletes- 

00:02:34.946 --> 00:02:38.533
- You said in air quotes. You couldn't take anything but

00:02:38.533 --> 00:02:40.702
was the thing still going on behind the scenes?

00:02:40.702 --> 00:02:42.996
- Absolutely. I made up my mind. I really wanted it.

00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:45.707
I made the commitment, told them I wasn't gonna play basketball.

00:02:45.707 --> 00:02:50.086
And then, the next month, the coach called me into his office and said,

00:02:50.086 --> 00:02:52.255
"I gotta, uh, tell you something." I was like, "What is it?" He said,

00:02:52.255 --> 00:02:56.176
"You've been one of the few freshmen in
the history of the Playboy All American,

00:02:56.176 --> 00:02:59.888
that they're naming you to next year's Playboy All American team."

00:02:59.888 --> 00:03:04.309
I was like, "Really?" And he was like, “Yeah.” I was like, “What does it require?”

00:03:04.309 --> 00:03:07.770
And he said, “Well, they have a big photo shoot. It's gonna be in Miami.

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:09.480
We're gonna send someone down there with you."

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:10.398
- Ah, hell.

00:03:10.398 --> 00:03:15.528
- First trip to Miami. This is before
your day, but man, Miami Vice was hot.

00:03:15.528 --> 00:03:20.408
All right? Crockett, Tubbs. Bruh, they was it.

00:03:20.408 --> 00:03:23.119
And then they told us we were staying at the hotel

00:03:23.119 --> 00:03:25.121
where they were doing a lot of the filming and everything.

00:03:25.121 --> 00:03:25.997
- Wow.

00:03:25.997 --> 00:03:29.542
- Oh my goodness. So, we go to the photo shoot, and then,

00:03:29.542 --> 00:03:33.504
some of the older guys, they're gonna take me
under their wing. They introduce me to a guy.

00:03:33.504 --> 00:03:35.340
They say, "Man, I wanna introduce you to my guy."

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:41.512
That happened to be Lloyd Bloom, of
the legendary Lloyd and Norby Walters.

00:03:41.512 --> 00:03:45.433
They were kind of booking agents in the music industry

00:03:45.433 --> 00:03:50.271
and they had 10 of the top 15 Black artists in R&amp;B

00:03:50.271 --> 00:03:52.899
and they were trying to make a mark in sports.

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:53.441
- Wow.

00:03:53.441 --> 00:03:55.443
- It was like, "Wow, this could be good."

00:03:55.443 --> 00:03:58.780
They was talking about marketing the players with the bands,

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.241
going to see all these acts. And I was like,

00:04:01.241 --> 00:04:04.827
"If I could just go to all these concerts, I'm
gonna be cool, if I don't do nothing else."

00:04:04.827 --> 00:04:10.416
And I really thought, at that time, I was
doing something cutting edge, ahead of.

00:04:10.416 --> 00:04:13.836
Like, I knew this was a decision I was gonna have three years from now,

00:04:13.836 --> 00:04:19.550
but if I could take care of this now, and the
benefit of that, I think they gave me $15,000.

00:04:19.550 --> 00:04:20.718
- 15 racks?

00:04:20.718 --> 00:04:22.720
- Yeah. I remember picking them up in Columbus.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:25.056
That's where we signed the contract, like that next week.

00:04:25.056 --> 00:04:30.770
I can't remember if I'm driving or if I'm
in the back. I'm counting the money.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:32.355
- [Laughing]

00:04:32.355 --> 00:04:33.940
- It was all 100s. It was all 100s.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:38.486
So I think I got 10 to sign, five
later on, and then also I got a car,

00:04:38.486 --> 00:04:41.531
and then I got $500 a month that they Western Unioned.

00:04:41.531 --> 00:04:44.117
You know, people start thinking, "Well, $500 a month.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:46.452
I'm a freshman. I'm gonna have this for three years.”

00:04:46.452 --> 00:04:47.412
- Wow.

00:04:47.412 --> 00:04:51.040
- So I didn't have to worry about all that.

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:53.918
Immediately, they gave me an application for an American Express.

00:04:53.918 --> 00:04:57.797
I was like, "I ain't never even had a credit
card." I ain't even ever lived in a house.

00:04:57.797 --> 00:05:01.592
Man, I always lived in a housing project.

00:05:01.592 --> 00:05:02.552
That was a good conversation.

00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:04.554
-  You good. Oh, we just getting started, bro.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.599
Oo, you sure you ain’t play before?

00:05:08.599 --> 00:05:10.852
-  I didn't say I didn't play before. I just said I'm not that good at it.

00:05:10.852 --> 00:05:12.520
- No that was sweet. That was nice.

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.691
If the NIL rules existed today when you were playing in college,

00:05:16.691 --> 00:05:18.401
how much money you think you would've made?

00:05:18.401 --> 00:05:21.487
-  It's not a- How much trouble you think I'm gonna get into?

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.114
- So what, what did money do for you then?

00:05:23.114 --> 00:05:26.242
- The things I was doing, it just allowed me to do more of it.

00:05:26.242 --> 00:05:29.537
And we were just smoking weed, just a little bit.

00:05:29.537 --> 00:05:35.084
And that's just when cocaine, people
started smoking it, um, rocking it up.

00:05:35.084 --> 00:05:38.755
And people had just started putting it in marijuana.

00:05:38.755 --> 00:05:43.676
And, a guy at a party, man had put one in a
cigarette. And man when he did that it like,

00:05:43.676 --> 00:05:49.640
like a shot off, went in my head. And it was like,
I had never experienced anything like that.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.642
And the money…
- Did you know it was or no?

00:05:51.642 --> 00:05:56.356
- That was my first time ever ex- experiencing crack cocaine.

00:05:56.356 --> 00:06:01.819
And it blew my mind. Unfortunately,
that started when I was at Ohio State.

00:06:01.819 --> 00:06:05.948
- All the money and everything that came
with that Miami trip put you in that situation.

00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:09.702
How were you responding to that
over the following years after that?

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:14.290
- This is the thing, I don't know how I
played at such a high, high, high level.

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:17.543
My sophomore year I went on to break all the sophomore records.

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:20.421
Uh, my junior year I went on to break all the junior records.

00:06:20.421 --> 00:06:24.217
Every bowl game I played in I was MVP in that bowl game.

00:06:24.217 --> 00:06:26.636
And I don't say that from a bragging standpoint,

00:06:26.636 --> 00:06:30.306
It’s just, I don't know how I was able to function at that level.

00:06:30.306 --> 00:06:34.602
I was always on time, never had any disciplinary stuff.

00:06:34.602 --> 00:06:36.979
But ultimately, it's gonna catch up to you.

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:37.688
- Mhmm.

00:06:37.688 --> 00:06:39.774
- Yeah. To me, it's not about the money.

00:06:39.774 --> 00:06:42.610
I do believe that there's a way that we can compensate,

00:06:42.610 --> 00:06:48.324
but giving young people unlimited amounts of income is not the best thing.

00:06:48.324 --> 00:06:52.578
I'm not happy for the guys, cuz guys eventually gonna get their money.

00:06:52.578 --> 00:06:54.789
I'm more happy for the women. 

00:06:54.789 --> 00:06:56.332
And they're able to take advantage of it,
- Speak on it, bro.

00:06:56.332 --> 00:07:01.045
- TikTok, all these things. Sell them-
Like, yes, they can do it the right way.

00:07:01.045 --> 00:07:02.422
- What’s the advice you’d give them right now?

00:07:02.422 --> 00:07:05.550
In terms of how to navigate this space
now, being able to monetize all that like…

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:09.178
- I would say that there's really no experts in this area.

00:07:09.178 --> 00:07:12.974
And be very careful. The universities can recommend to the athlete

00:07:12.974 --> 00:07:15.435
a business or something that they can go to,

00:07:15.435 --> 00:07:19.564
but people are gonna make mistakes. This is new
territory, people are going to make mistakes.

00:07:19.564 --> 00:07:25.403
But, us thinking that young people are going to be better off

00:07:25.403 --> 00:07:29.615
with money than without it, that would be interesting to watch.

00:07:29.615 --> 00:07:30.825
- You speak on it first hand.

00:07:30.825 --> 00:07:32.743
- Yeah, yeah. Gator.

00:07:32.743 --> 00:07:36.080
